---
import Chapter from './Chapter.astro';
import Eq from './Eq.astro';
import Kind from './Kind.astro';
import Cite from './Cite.astro';
import Deep from './Deep.astro';
import data from '../../assets/generated/science/science.json';
import pixels from '../../assets/generated/science/pixels.png?url';
import planeR from '../../assets/generated/science/plane-r.webp?url';
import planeG from '../../assets/generated/science/plane-g.webp?url';
import planeB from '../../assets/generated/science/plane-b.webp?url';

const crop = data.pixels;
---

<section class="sci-sec" aria-labelledby="pixels">
  <div class="wrap">
    <Chapter
      n="01"
      id="pixels"
      scale="Scale: one pixel"
      title="A photograph is a table of numbers."
      lede="Zoom in far enough and every image dissolves into a grid of coloured squares. Each square is three integers. Everything CutOut does is arithmetic on those integers."
    />

    <figure class="lab" data-sci="pixels" data-src={pixels} data-x0={crop.left} data-y0={crop.top} data-fw={data.open.w}>
      <div class="lab__stage lab__stage--px">
        <canvas class="px__canvas" width="640" height="480" data-px-canvas aria-label="Magnified photo of the puppy's eye and nose. Use the arrow keys to move the selected pixel." tabindex="0" role="img"></canvas>
        <img class="lab__fallback" src={pixels} width={crop.width} height={crop.height} alt="A 256 by 192 pixel crop of the puppy's eye and nose." loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </div>
      <div class="lab__panel" hidden data-lab-panel>
        <div class="readout" aria-live="polite" data-px-readout>
          <p class="readout__k">Selected pixel</p>
          <p class="readout__xy tabular"><span data-px-x>–</span>, <span data-px-y>–</span></p>
          <div class="readout__swatch" data-px-swatch></div>
          <math class="readout__math" display="block" aria-hidden="true">
            <mrow>
              <mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo>
              <mrow>
                <mo>[</mo>
                <mtable>
                  <mtr><mtd><mn class="v-r" data-px-r>0</mn></mtd></mtr>
                  <mtr><mtd><mn class="v-g" data-px-g>0</mn></mtd></mtr>
                  <mtr><mtd><mn class="v-bl" data-px-b>0</mn></mtd></mtr>
                </mtable>
                <mo>]</mo>
              </mrow>
            </mrow>
          </math>
          <div class="bars" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="bar bar--r"><span data-px-bar="r"></span></span>
            <span class="bar bar--g"><span data-px-bar="g"></span></span>
            <span class="bar bar--b"><span data-px-bar="b"></span></span>
          </div>
          <p class="readout__hex tabular" data-px-hex></p>
        </div>

        <div class="ctl">
          <label class="ctl__row" for="px-zoom"><span>Magnification</span><output class="tabular" data-px-zoom-out>×4</output></label>
          <input id="px-zoom" class="range" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="36" data-px-zoom />
        </div>
        <div class="ctl">
          <span class="ctl__row" id="px-ch-label"><span>Channels</span></span>
          <div class="chips" role="group" aria-labelledby="px-ch-label">
            <button type="button" class="chip chip--r" aria-pressed="true" data-px-ch="0">Red</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip chip--g" aria-pressed="true" data-px-ch="1">Green</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip chip--b" aria-pressed="true" data-px-ch="2">Blue</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="ctl">
          <label class="ctl__row" for="px-res"><span>Resolution</span><output class="tabular" data-px-res-out>Full</output></label>
          <input id="px-res" class="range" type="range" min="0" max="5" step="1" value="0" data-px-res />
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="lab__cap">
        <Kind k="app" /> A lossless 256 × 192 crop of the photograph above, decoded exactly as an image file is decoded. Choose a pixel with a click, a tap or the arrow keys; switch channels off to see how the three
        numbers mix; lower the resolution to average blocks of pixels together <Kind k="concept" />.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="prose-grid">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A colour photograph is a function from positions to colours. CutOut stores it the way almost every image library does: row after row, four bytes per pixel for red, green, blue and alpha
          (opacity), each between 0 and 255. <Kind k="app" />
        </p>
        <Eq display n={1} tex={String.raw`\begin{aligned}I(x,y)&=\begin{bmatrix}\cR{R(x,y)}\\ \cG{G(x,y)}\\ \cBl{B(x,y)}\end{bmatrix}\\[4pt] &\in\{0,1,\dots,255\}^{3}\end{aligned}`} />
        <p>
          with <Eq tex={String.raw`0\le x<W`} /> and <Eq tex={String.raw`0\le y<H`} />. Here <Eq tex="x" /> counts columns from the left edge and <Eq tex="y" /> counts rows from the top — down, not up, unlike a school graph. The byte for channel <Eq tex="c" /> of pixel
          <Eq tex="(x,y)" /> sits at offset <Eq tex="4(yW+x)+c" />.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figure class="tensor">
      <div class="tensor__planes" aria-hidden="true">
        <img class="tensor__plane tensor__plane--r" src={planeR} width="320" height="320" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <img class="tensor__plane tensor__plane--g" src={planeG} width="320" height="320" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <img class="tensor__plane tensor__plane--b" src={planeB} width="320" height="320" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        <span class="tensor__dim tensor__dim--w tabular">1024</span>
        <span class="tensor__dim tensor__dim--h tabular">1024</span>
        <span class="tensor__dim tensor__dim--c tabular">3</span>
      </div>
      <div class="tensor__text">
        <p class="tensor__k">From photograph to tensor <Kind k="app" /><Cite ids="cutout-src" /></p>
        <ol class="steps" role="list">
          <li><span class="steps__n tabular">1</span><span>Any existing transparency is flattened onto neutral grey (128), so hidden pixels cannot confuse the network.</span></li>
          <li><span class="steps__n tabular">2</span><span>The image is resampled to 1024 × 1024 with a triangle filter — squeezed if it is not square.</span></li>
          <li><span class="steps__n tabular">3</span><span>Each channel is scaled to 0–1 and standardised with the ImageNet mean and spread the weights were trained with.<Cite ids="birefnet-weights" /></span></li>
          <li><span class="steps__n tabular">4</span><span>The three channels are split into separate planes: a tensor of 1 × 3 × 1024 × 1024 = 3,145,728 numbers.</span></li>
        </ol>
        <Eq display n={2} tex={String.raw`\begin{aligned}\hat{x}_{c}(i,j)&=\frac{x_{c}(i,j)/255-\mu_{c}}{\sigma_{c}}\\[4pt] \boldsymbol{\mu}&=(0.485,\,0.456,\,0.406)\\ \boldsymbol{\sigma}&=(0.229,\,0.224,\,0.225)\end{aligned}`} />
      </div>
    </figure>

    <div class="deeps">
      <Deep level="technical" title="Why squeeze the photo into a square?">
        <p>
          The network was trained on 1024 × 1024 inputs, and both of CutOut's runtimes compile it for exactly that shape: Core ML with a static input shape, ONNX Runtime with the shape stored in the
          model file. CutOut therefore does not pad or crop. It resamples the whole photo to the square, accepting a temporary change of proportions, and later stretches the prediction back to the
          photo's own size. <Kind k="app" />
        </p>
        <p>
          The result is that a 36-megapixel portrait and a 0.9-megapixel bicycle cost the network the same work. On CutOut's reference Mac, inference time does not depend on the photo's size. Only the
          final full-resolution steps do. <Kind k="app" />
        </p>
        <p>
          In memory the tensor is <em>planar</em> (all red values, then all green, then all blue), whereas the photo was <em>interleaved</em> (R, G, B, A for one pixel, then the next). At 4 bytes
          per number that is 12 MiB of FP32 input, and half that when the model takes FP16.
        </p>
      </Deep>
      <Deep level="maths" title="Resampling with a triangle filter">
        <p>
          Each output pixel is a weighted average of the input pixels near its centre. CutOut's resampler (the Rust <code>image</code> library) uses the triangle, or tent, kernel. When it shrinks an
          image by a factor <Eq tex="s>1" />, it widens the kernel by the same factor, so that every input pixel contributes. <Kind k="app" />
        </p>
        <Eq display n={3} tex={String.raw`\begin{aligned}\tilde{x}(u)&=\frac{\sum_{k} w\!\left(\tfrac{u_{0}-k}{s}\right)x(k)}{\sum_{k} w\!\left(\tfrac{u_{0}-k}{s}\right)}\\[4pt] w(t)&=\max(0,\,1-|t|),\qquad u_{0}=s\left(u+\tfrac12\right)-\tfrac12\end{aligned}`} />
        <p>
          The filter is applied horizontally and then vertically, because the two-dimensional kernel is the product of two one-dimensional ones. Flattening transparency onto grey happens before
          resampling. For each channel <Eq tex="c" /> with straight alpha <Eq tex="a" />:
        </p>
        <Eq display n={4} tex={String.raw`c'=\left\lfloor\frac{c\,a+128\,(255-a)+127}{255}\right\rfloor`} />
      </Deep>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
